6.1 Content Versioning

The framework maintains historical versions of crawled content rather than silently overwriting previous data. Each update is stored with a timestamp, allowing changes over time to be tracked and audited.
ex:
Version 1
Distributed scraping with Celery workers

Version 2
Distributed scraping with Celery workers and Redis

Version 3
Distributed scraping with Celery workers, Redis and Qdrant


7. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)

The framework implements a Retrieval-Augmented Generation pipeline.

Embedding Generation

SentenceTransformer model: all-MiniLM-L6-v2 is used to generate vector embeddings.

Retrieval

User questions are converted into vector embeddings and matched against stored document vectors inside Qdrant.

Generation

The retrieved context is passed to:

google/flan-t5-base

which generates a final answer.

Figure 2. Retrieval-Augmented Generation Workflow.

7.1 Chunking Strategy

The framework implements an overlap-based chunking strategy before indexing content into the vector database.

Instead of embedding entire documents as a single vector, documents are divided into overlapping chunks. This approach preserves context between neighboring chunks while improving retrieval accuracy.

example:
Chunk 1:
Distributed scraping with Celery workers...

Chunk 2:
Redis manages task queues...

Chunk 3:
Qdrant stores vector embeddings...

Benefits
Preserves contextual continuity.
Reduces information loss.
Improves semantic retrieval.
Enhances RAG performance.

7.2 Multi-Source Retrieval and Context Generation

The retrieval component is configured to retrieve multiple relevant documents from Qdrant for each query.

Retrieved sources are combined into a shared context before being passed to the FLAN‑T5 language model. This enables generated answers to incorporate information from multiple retrieved sources rather than relying on a single document.

Workflow:
Question
↓
Generate Query Embedding
↓
Retrieve Top-K Sources
↓
Merge Retrieved Context
↓
FLAN-T5
↓
Generated Answer + Citations

Benefits:
Supports information synthesis.
Improves answer completeness.
Reduces reliance on a single source.
Increases retrieval robustness.

