10. Challenges and Solutions
Docker Dependency Conflicts

During backend containerization, dependency conflicts involving Python packages and Qdrant-related components were encountered. Infrastructure services such as Redis, PostgreSQL, and Qdrant were successfully deployed through Docker.

Hugging Face Model Integration

FLAN‑T5 required downloading and configuring a local language model before successful integration into the RAG pipeline.

Retrieval-Augmented Generation

Developing an effective retrieval pipeline required integrating embeddings, vector search, chunking, multi-source retrieval, and answer generation into a single workflow.

Distributed Processing

Implementing reliable task processing required retries, fault handling, and the introduction of a dead-letter queue for repeatedly failing tasks.

10.1 Technology Selection Rationale
Python

Python was selected because it provides mature libraries for web scraping, machine learning, distributed processing, and API development.

Alternative considered:

Node.js

Python was chosen due to stronger ecosystem support for Scrapy, SentenceTransformer, and machine learning libraries

Scrapy

Scrapy was selected as the primary crawling framework because it provides high-performance crawling, request scheduling, and built-in support for large-scale scraping.

Alternative considered:

BeautifulSoup

BeautifulSoup was not selected because it is a parsing library rather than a complete crawling framework.

Playwright

Playwright was chosen to handle JavaScript-rendered websites.

Alternative considered:

Selenium

Playwright was selected because it offers modern browser automation, improved performance, and simpler integration.

Redis and Celery

Redis and Celery were selected to support distributed task execution and asynchronous processing.

Alternative considered:

RabbitMQ

Redis was chosen due to its simpler setup and integration with Celery.

PostgreSQL

PostgreSQL was chosen for metadata storage because of its reliability and strong support for structured data.

Alternative considered:

MongoDB

PostgreSQL was preferred due to its transactional consistency and relational model.

Qdrant

Qdrant was selected as the vector database for semantic search and retrieval.

Alternative considered:

FAISS

Qdrant was chosen because it provides a dedicated vector database with persistence and API support.

FastAPI

FastAPI was used to build the backend API because of its performance and automatic Swagger documentation.

Alternative considered:

Flask

FastAPI was chosen due to built-in validation and API documentation support.

FLAN-T5

FLAN‑T5 was selected as the language model for answer generation.

Alternative considered:

OpenAI GPT models

FLAN‑T5 was chosen because it can be executed locally without external API dependency.

11. Conclusion

A Distributed Retrieval-Augmented Generation Scraper Framework was successfully designed and implemented.

The framework integrates:

Scrapy
Playwright
Redis
Celery
PostgreSQL
Qdrant
FastAPI
React/Vite
SentenceTransformer
FLAN‑T5

The final implementation supports distributed web scraping, JavaScript-rendered content extraction, semantic indexing, deduplication, incremental crawling, content versioning, fault tolerance through dead-letter queues, semantic retrieval, and retrieval‑augmented question answering with source attribution.

The completed system demonstrates a scalable architecture capable of collecting, processing, indexing, and querying web content through both API and web-based interfaces.